      name: 'Watch the number while you build, then take the instrument out',
      does: 'A `print()` in `Start()` reports the budget you begin with. It is a tool for one afternoon, not a feature — the Console line it costs you every jump is not something a finished game owes anybody.',
      doc: '`print()` from 1.3',
      href: '1.3.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Four of the five parts are work you have already done: two `int`s are 1.3, the jump is 6.1, the ' +
    'callback is 2.1, and a tag is 2.1 again. The new idea is narrow and worth saying out loud — **a ' +
    'limit is only a limit if something refills it**. A counter that only ever goes down is not a ' +
    'jump limit, it is a countdown to standing still forever.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one search? "Stop the infinite ' +
    'jump" cannot be looked up. "Do something when the player collides with the ground" can.',
  turns: [
    'Your budget is refilled by a message from the physics engine, not by asking a question every frame. Name one situation where the message arrives late, or does not arrive at all, and the player would tell you the game is broken.',
    '`maxJumps` is 1. Before you change it to 2, say what else in this lesson would have to be true for a second jump to actually be a second jump.',
  ],
};

const WALK = {
  ask: 'why can I jump forever in mid-air?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs',
  label: 'In the Code',
  intro:
    'Four small changes to the file you already have. Three are shapes from earlier in the course — two fields, a compound condition, a collision callback. One line is yours: the refill. Get it wrong and the jetpack comes back with no error message to explain why.',
  level: 2,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Add the budget: how many, and how many left',
      lead: 'Two lines in the class body, beside `jumpForce` from 6.1.',
      lines: [
        { code: '    int jumpsRemaining;', what: 'How many jumps are left **right now**. No initializer: a field of type `int` starts at `0`, and you are about to fill it in `Start()`.', why: '**Why not just start it at 1?** Because a value that is set in two places (`Start()` and the landing) is a value you can forget to reset in one of them. Give it one source of truth — `maxJumps` — and set it from that everywhere.' },
        { code: '    int maxJumps = 1;', what: '**How many jumps you are allowed between landings.** `1` is an ordinary jump. `2` is a double jump, and that is the whole of 6.4: one number changing.', why: '**The limit lives in its own field, not in the condition.** Written as `jumpsRemaining >= 1` the `1` is hidden inside the gate; written as `maxJumps` the number has a name you can find, change and explain.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Fill it when the scene starts',
      lead: 'In the `else` branch of `Start()` — the platformer branch you wrote in 6.1.',
      lines: [
        { code: '            jumpsRemaining = maxJumps;', what: '**One jump, on the floor, before you press anything.** Without this line the game starts at `0`, the first jump never fires, and nothing about the code looks wrong.', why: '**This is the same sentence as the landing, said once at the beginning.** A field that gets its value from another field is telling you where the real number lives.' },
        { code: '            print(jumpsRemaining);', what: '**An instrument, not a feature.** One line in the Console at the start of the cave, so you can see the budget you begin with. Delete it once the number is right — or leave it and be able to say why.', why: '**Not a `Debug.LogError()`.** This is not a failure; the game is working. A red line in the Console means "something is wrong", and spending that colour on a number you are watching trains you to ignore it.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Make the jump cost one',
      lead: 'In `Update()`, the platformer branch — the `else` where `linearVelocityX` is set.',
      lines: [
        { code: '            if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.J) && jumpsRemaining >= 1)', what: '**The jump from 6.1 with one term added.** `GetKeyDown` is still what makes one press one jump; `jumpsRemaining >= 1` is the new question — do I have any left? Read it in English before you trust it: "jump if the key went down this frame **and** I have a jump to spend."', why: '**`>= 1`, not `> 0`.** They mean the same thing here, and both are correct. Pick one and be able to say why: `>= 1` reads as "at least one", which is what the player is thinking.' },
        { code: '            {', what: '' },
        { code: '                Jump();', what: 'The method from 6.1, unchanged. The force, the `ForceMode2D.Impulse`, the `print` inside it — none of it moves today.' },
        { code: '                jumpsRemaining -= 1;', what: '**Spend it, inside the `if`.** Inside is the whole point: a jump you were not allowed to take must not cost you one. Put this line above the `if` and a mid-air key press quietly drains a budget it never spent.', why: '**`-= 1` is `jumpsRemaining = jumpsRemaining - 1`.** A shorter way to say the same thing, and it reads left to right as "take one away", which is what the player just did.' },
        { code: '            }', what: '' },
        { code: '            rb.linearVelocityX = xVector;', what: 'Your horizontal movement from 6.1, untouched. It sits **outside** the `if` on purpose — you can always walk, whether or not you have a jump.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Refill it when you land — this is the blank',
      lead: 'A new method, anywhere in the class beside `Update()`. This is the only line today with nothing earlier in the course to copy.',
      lines: [
        { code: '    void OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D other)', what: '**Not a trigger.** The floor is solid and so is your collider, so the engine reports a *collision*, not a trigger — and the parameter is a `Collision2D` rather than a `Collider2D`. Same two callbacks you met in 2.1, in their solid shape.', why: '**`Enter`, not `Stay`.** This fires on the frame contact begins and then stops. `Stay` would fire every physics step you stood on the floor, which costs nothing here and would matter a lot if the body of this method ever did more than set one number.' },
        { code: '    {', what: '' },
        { code: '        if (other.gameObject.CompareTag("Ground"))', what: '**Only the ground.** Everything solid is not ground: your cave has a second solid surface that carries no `Ground` tag, and this line is what makes that difference matter.' },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        { code: '', blank: true, what: '**Put the budget back.** One statement, using the two fields from step 1: the one you spend from gets the value of the one that sets the limit. Do not write a number here — if you write `= 1` you have made `maxJumps` a lie, and the double jump in 6.4 will not work no matter what you set it to.', hint: 'The field that counts down is assigned the field that holds the maximum. Same line as the one you wrote in `Start()`, for the same reason — one source of truth for "how many you get".', why: '**This line is what makes the other three mean something.** Without it the player jumps once per scene and then walks. A budget with no refill is not a limit, it is a one-way door.' },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
        { code: '    }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
