  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;
// ... plus UnityEngine.SceneManagement and System, from 6.1 and 3.5 ...

public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    // ... speed, direction, animator, renderer, cooldownTimer, rb ...

    [SerializeField] float jumpForce;     // from 6.1 — the number you tuned
    bool isPlatformer = false;            // from 6.1 — set from the scene name

    int jumpsRemaining;                   // how many are left right now
    int maxJumps = 1;                     // how many you get between landings

    private void Start()
    {
        // ... the component lookups and the StatManager check ...

        Scene currentScene = SceneManager.GetActiveScene();
        if (currentScene.name == "Overworld")
        {
            rb.gravityScale = 0;
            isPlatformer = false;
        }
        else
        {
            rb.gravityScale = 1;
            isPlatformer = true;
            jumpsRemaining = maxJumps;    // you start on the floor
            print(jumpsRemaining);        // an instrument — delete when it is right
        }
    }

    private void Update()
    {
        // ... input, the facing block, the sword and the bullet ...

        if (!isPlatformer)
        {
            rb.linearVelocity = new Vector2(xVector, yVector);
        }
        else
        {
            if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.J) && jumpsRemaining >= 1)
            {
                Jump();
                jumpsRemaining -= 1;      // spend it only if you jumped
            }
            rb.linearVelocityX = xVector;
        }

        // ... cooldownTimer, the sword, the bullet, UpdateAnimation() ...
    }

    void OnCollisionEnter2D(Collision2D other)
    {
        if (other.gameObject.CompareTag("Ground"))
        {
            jumpsRemaining = maxJumps;    // landed: the budget is back
        }
    }

    void Jump()
    {
        print("jump");
        rb.AddForceY(jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse);
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare against your own file rather than copying it. The line that matters is the refill: `jumpsRemaining = maxJumps` — written from the field, never from a literal. The other three are the budget, the gate and the spend, and they do nothing useful without it.',
};

const body = `<h1>6.2 — Ground Checks: No Infinite Jumps</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your jump works. That is the problem. Tap the key in mid-air and you jump again, and again —
    a jump with no ground check is not a jump, it is a jetpack with infinite fuel. Today you give
    the script the one thing it has never had: <em>a budget</em>, and a way to refill it.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('why can I jump forever in mid-air?', 2)}

${renderDecompose('6.2', DECOMPOSE, 2)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> open <code>Assets/_Scenes/Platformer.unity</code>,
            then <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play and keep tapping the jump key in mid-air.
            You climb, and the Console says nothing — your code is doing exactly what it says:
            <em>if the key went down, add an impulse</em>. There is no rule about where.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → Grid → Collision</strong>, then <strong>Inspector →</strong> a
            <strong>TilemapCollider2D</strong>, a <strong>CompositeCollider2D</strong> and a
            <strong>Rigidbody2D</strong> whose <strong>Body Type is Static</strong> — which is why
            you land on it. Its <strong>Gravity Scale</strong> reads <code>1</code> and is
            <strong>greyed out</strong>, and both are correct: only a Dynamic body is moved by the
            engine, so there is nothing for that number to act on. <strong>Do not "fix" the 1</strong>
            — switch this tilemap to Dynamic and the ground falls out of the level.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → Grid → Collision → Inspector → Tag</strong>: it already reads
            <code>Ground</code>. Open <strong>Project Settings ▸ Tags and Layers</strong> and confirm
            it is in the list — the tag exists in this project, so nobody has to create it today.
            What you are checking is that the <em>floor</em> carries it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → Grid → Cave Exit → Inspector → Tag</strong>: <strong>Untagged</strong>,
            and solid, and carrying <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> — touching it sends you back to the
            Overworld. Two surfaces you can stand on, one of which is not ground. Hold that thought
            for the trap below.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window → Assets/Prefabs/Player</strong>, then
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> the <strong>Rigidbody2D</strong> (Dynamic, gravity on) and
            the <strong>BoxCollider2D</strong> you added back in 2.1 and 2.2. Those two are the
            reason the engine has anything to report to you at all — a callback is only sent to a
            body with a collider on it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> play, land on the floor, then walk off a ledge and land on
            the cave mouth. When the walkthrough is done, one of those landings will give your jump
            back and the other will not.
        </li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>The cave mouth is solid, and it is not ground.</strong> <code>Cave Exit</code> has the
        same three components as the floor and none of its tag, so landing on it does not refill your
        budget. Your code is working correctly and the player will still tell you the game is broken.
        That is the honest cost of a check built on a name: a tag answers <em>"is this that one
        particular thing?"</em>, and every new surface has to be told the answer by hand. A later
        lesson comes back to this with a raycast and a layer, where the engine filters for you.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Asking Versus Being Told</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Your budget is refilled by a message from the physics engine rather than by asking "am I on the ground?" every frame. Name one moment where the message arrives late, and what the player sees when it does.</li>
            <li>The cave mouth is solid but untagged, so landing on it does not give your jump back. Is that a bug in your script or in your level — and which one would you change first?</li>
            <li><code>maxJumps</code> is <code>1</code> today. If you changed it to <code>2</code> right now, would you get a double jump? Name every line that would have to be true for the second jump to actually work.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        There are three common ways to answer <em>"am I standing on something?"</em>, and this
        project builds the first. Read all three — the differences are in <em>when</em> each one
        knows, and what it can tell you when it does.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Technique</th><th>When it knows</th><th>What it can tell you</th><th>Where it lies to you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnCollisionEnter2D()</code> plus <code>CompareTag()</code></td>
            <td>On the frame contact begins — a message from the engine.</td>
            <td>That you touched <em>something</em>, and what that something is named.</td>
            <td>Nothing fires when you <em>leave</em>, so the budget stays full until you land again; and every new surface needs the tag by hand.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnTriggerEnter2D()</code> on a small child under your feet</td>
            <td>On the frame the child's trigger overlaps something.</td>
            <td>That a particular point on your body is touching something.</td>
            <td>The child has to be placed by hand, and a trigger that starts inside a collider reports it forever.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Physics2D.Raycast()</code> every frame</td>
            <td>Whenever you ask — every frame, for as long as you want.</td>
            <td>Whether something is under one exact point, <em>how far</em> away it is, and what it is.</td>
            <td>An infinitely thin line: a pixel of your foot over a ledge reports air. It also needs a <code>LayerMask</code> to filter what it may notice.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        The third row is where this course goes next — <a href="7.2.html">7.2</a> replaces the tag
        check with a raycast and a layer, which is what fixes the cave mouth without you tagging
        anything. Today is the honest first version, and it is what ships in most small games.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
