    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — watch the number.</strong> Keep the <code>print()</code> in
        <code>Start()</code> for now and add one to the refill for a minute. Press Play and jump:
        the Console should read the budget going down by one on every jump, and back to the maximum
        on every landing. Take both prints out before you commit.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — the spend that is in the wrong place.</strong> Move
        <code>jumpsRemaining -= 1;</code> above the <code>if</code> and play. You cannot jump at all:
        every key press spends a jump you never took. Put it back inside, and say out loud why the
        position is the whole difference.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 4 — the refill that is hard-coded.</strong> Change
        <code>jumpsRemaining = maxJumps;</code> to <code>jumpsRemaining = 1;</code>, then set
        <code>maxJumps = 2</code> and play. You still only get one jump per landing. That is exactly
        the bug that would make 6.4 impossible, and it is a one-word fix.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the budget.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Where does the number of allowed jumps live, and how many places read it? A limit written into a condition is a limit nobody can tune.</li>
            <li>Is the spend inside the same condition as the jump? Say what the player experiences when it is not.</li>
            <li>Which surfaces refill the budget, and how does the code know? Name the mechanism — a tag, a layer, a component — and one surface it gets wrong.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

